1
00:00:04,872 --> 00:00:06,540
I'm scared, Joe.

2
00:00:06,674 --> 00:00:08,076
I'm scared for you.

3
00:00:08,176 --> 00:00:10,178
I'm scared for our daughters.

4
00:00:10,278 --> 00:00:12,613
♪ tense music ♪

5
00:00:12,713 --> 00:00:13,847
[Joe]
What did you sell them?

6
00:00:13,947 --> 00:00:15,283
[Cade]
Whatever he's looking for.

7
00:00:15,383 --> 00:00:17,051
He owns my fucking degree.
My whole future.

8
00:00:17,185 --> 00:00:18,586
Not anymore, he doesn't.

9
00:00:18,686 --> 00:00:20,088
We hold it now.

10
00:00:20,188 --> 00:00:22,722
<i>So you get something,
you send him a text,</i>

11
00:00:22,723 --> 00:00:25,025
<i>he sends you an address
and you meet him there.</i>

12
00:00:25,126 --> 00:00:26,026
<i>- Is that it?</i>
- [Cade] <i>Yes.</i>

13
00:00:26,294 --> 00:00:27,695
♪

14
00:00:27,795 --> 00:00:30,298
Green light.
Green light, let's go.

15
00:00:30,664 --> 00:00:32,500
What the fuck!? [yells]

16
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,402
[whimpering]

17
00:00:36,304 --> 00:00:37,738
When you're found guilty,

18
00:00:37,838 --> 00:00:39,573
life imprisonment times three.

19
00:00:39,673 --> 00:00:40,841
Understand the game?

20
00:00:40,941 --> 00:00:43,211
Trade information for freedom.

21
00:00:43,311 --> 00:00:45,579
We began our operation

22
00:00:45,679 --> 00:00:48,249
targeting known assets
of Russian Intelligence.

23
00:00:48,349 --> 00:00:49,583
Which brings us to the question,

24
00:00:49,717 --> 00:00:51,018
is our national security
at risk?

25
00:00:51,119 --> 00:00:52,120
Without a question.

26
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:54,588
♪ dramatic music ♪

27
00:00:55,489 --> 00:00:56,624
[exhales]

28
00:00:56,890 --> 00:00:58,626
♪

29
00:00:58,726 --> 00:01:00,060
[muffled grunting]

30
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:03,564
♪

31
00:01:04,832 --> 00:01:07,101
♪ tense, somber music ♪

32
00:01:12,573 --> 00:01:14,775
[helicopter whirring]

33
00:01:17,911 --> 00:01:20,413
[Cruz] Getting real fucking
bored of this stateside gig.

34
00:01:20,414 --> 00:01:21,948
What are you talking about?

35
00:01:21,949 --> 00:01:24,285
You just karate-chopped
some fucker last night.

36
00:01:24,418 --> 00:01:25,786
- The schoolteacher?
- Yeah.

37
00:01:25,919 --> 00:01:27,588
[clicks tongue]
Fuck that. That ain't a fight.

38
00:01:27,721 --> 00:01:31,259
[laughs softly] She's got a lot
of pent-up aggression, this one.

39
00:01:31,392 --> 00:01:34,328
[chuckles]
We are all very aware of that.

40
00:01:34,428 --> 00:01:36,530
Hey, we go
wherever the fight is.

41
00:01:37,265 --> 00:01:39,967
Right now, it has us here, so...

42
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:43,337
I mean, shit, I don't mind
a few soft targets,

43
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:46,207
'cause you know
the hard targets are coming.

44
00:01:47,641 --> 00:01:49,076
They always do.

45
00:01:49,177 --> 00:01:51,779
<i>Agents of FBI's
counterintelligence unit</i>

46
00:01:51,912 --> 00:01:53,614
<i>engaged in an operation</i>

47
00:01:53,714 --> 00:01:55,782
<i>that spanned five states</i>

48
00:01:55,783 --> 00:01:57,485
<i>and resulted in multiple arrests</i>

49
00:01:57,618 --> 00:01:59,652
<i>in what the Department
of Justice is claiming</i>

50
00:01:59,653 --> 00:02:02,490
<i>represents the largest
sting operation</i>

51
00:02:02,623 --> 00:02:05,293
<i>since the Abscam operation
in 1978,</i>

52
00:02:05,393 --> 00:02:07,395
<i>which led to the arrest
and conviction</i>

53
00:02:07,495 --> 00:02:08,962
<i>of seven members of Congress,</i>

54
00:02:09,062 --> 00:02:11,131
<i>- the mayor of Camden...</i>
- Well, you weren't kidding.

55
00:02:11,265 --> 00:02:13,133
<i>...and three Philadelphia
City Council members.</i>

56
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:17,170
<i>While no public officials have
been arrested to this point...</i>

57
00:02:17,171 --> 00:02:19,807
What have I told you
about the news, babe?

58
00:02:19,907 --> 00:02:22,309
<i>- ...high-ranking members...</i>
- It's a boat fire.

59
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:23,777
Boat fire?

60
00:02:24,578 --> 00:02:26,680
What do the rats do
when the boat's on fire?

61
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,649
<i>- ...have been arrested.</i>
- They jump ship.

62
00:02:28,782 --> 00:02:30,451
So, you're telling me
they weren't really arrested?

63
00:02:30,551 --> 00:02:32,152
Oh, yeah, they were arrested.

64
00:02:32,253 --> 00:02:34,855
So, that... it's true?

65
00:02:34,955 --> 00:02:36,190
It's a sliver of the truth.

66
00:02:36,324 --> 00:02:38,526
Now the news will try
to discredit it.

67
00:02:38,626 --> 00:02:39,993
- Well, why would they do that?
- [laughs softly]

68
00:02:40,127 --> 00:02:42,029
'Cause it's what they do.

69
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:44,332
The news is the propaganda arm
of whoever

70
00:02:44,432 --> 00:02:47,668
has the greatest influence over
the organization reporting it.

71
00:02:47,768 --> 00:02:51,538
[exhales] Wow. When did
they get control of the news?

72
00:02:51,539 --> 00:02:53,006
It's always been this way.

73
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:54,575
I mean,
read the <i>New York Times</i> version

74
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:56,042
of the Battle of Gettysburg,

75
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:57,845
and then a newspaper
in Atlanta's version

76
00:02:57,945 --> 00:03:00,179
and tell me
it's the same battle.

77
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:01,915
They call it "stories"
for a reason.

78
00:03:04,084 --> 00:03:07,154
Honey. Are we close?

79
00:03:08,389 --> 00:03:10,458
To this being over for us?

80
00:03:12,226 --> 00:03:13,561
We're close.

81
00:03:20,468 --> 00:03:22,535
[Joe]
How we doing?

82
00:03:22,536 --> 00:03:24,137
Peachy.

83
00:03:25,005 --> 00:03:26,374
Yeah?

84
00:03:28,409 --> 00:03:30,811
[stifled laugh]
Don't look too peachy.

85
00:03:31,645 --> 00:03:34,114
Yeah, we're, uh,
peachy on the inside.

86
00:03:40,153 --> 00:03:42,756
What if I told you guys...

87
00:03:43,491 --> 00:03:45,926
...that we can go home soon?

88
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:47,728
How peachy would you be then?

89
00:03:47,861 --> 00:03:50,398
- Really?
- Mm-hmm. Really.

90
00:03:51,198 --> 00:03:52,766
Uh, is it safe?

91
00:03:52,866 --> 00:03:54,901
Honey, I wouldn't take us home
if it wasn't.

92
00:03:54,902 --> 00:03:56,670
When do we get to go home?

93
00:03:57,505 --> 00:04:00,808
In a few days, I think.

94
00:04:04,478 --> 00:04:07,014
I know it's been hard,

95
00:04:07,114 --> 00:04:08,916
and I'm sorry.

96
00:04:09,049 --> 00:04:11,585
You guys have been
such troopers.

97
00:04:16,424 --> 00:04:18,426
Do you ever think about them?

98
00:04:18,526 --> 00:04:19,860
Who?

99
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:21,961
The men that came to our house.

100
00:04:21,962 --> 00:04:24,197
♪ slow, somber music ♪

101
00:04:27,635 --> 00:04:30,303
- Not at all?
- Not for a second.

102
00:04:30,304 --> 00:04:33,341
And I don't want you
to think about them, either.

103
00:04:34,274 --> 00:04:36,243
They don't deserve your fear.

104
00:04:36,977 --> 00:04:40,013
And they forfeited their right
to your compassion.

105
00:04:42,783 --> 00:04:45,419
The only thing they deserve

106
00:04:45,519 --> 00:04:47,621
is to be forgotten.

107
00:04:53,627 --> 00:04:55,896
♪ tense, atmospheric music ♪

108
00:04:59,333 --> 00:05:01,535
[loud chatter]

109
00:05:06,807 --> 00:05:08,576
[sighs]

110
00:05:09,443 --> 00:05:10,978
- Hey.
- [Joe] Good morning.

111
00:05:11,078 --> 00:05:12,546
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

112
00:05:17,918 --> 00:05:19,151
[sighs]

113
00:05:19,152 --> 00:05:20,988
This is my favorite place
in D.C.

114
00:05:21,088 --> 00:05:22,990
- Really?
- Yeah.

115
00:05:23,824 --> 00:05:26,994
[Mullins] Name another saloon
that serves eggs Chesapeake.

116
00:05:27,795 --> 00:05:30,097
Hell, name another saloon
that serves breakfast,

117
00:05:30,197 --> 00:05:32,866
- for that matter.
- [chuckles]

118
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:35,669
McKinley, Grant...

119
00:05:35,769 --> 00:05:38,671
- Mm.
- ...Cleveland, Roosevelt.

120
00:05:38,672 --> 00:05:42,209
[Mullins] Every one of them
stood at that bar.

121
00:05:43,043 --> 00:05:45,345
Fitting place
to end the career of a man

122
00:05:45,446 --> 00:05:47,847
who has forsaken the oath
he swore--

123
00:05:47,848 --> 00:05:50,684
that we all swore-- to uphold.

124
00:05:51,452 --> 00:05:55,355
I'd be lying if I said I wasn't
really looking forward to this.

125
00:05:55,489 --> 00:05:57,056
[Meade chuckles]

126
00:05:57,057 --> 00:05:58,358
[door opens]

127
00:06:03,363 --> 00:06:04,565
I'll start us off,

128
00:06:04,665 --> 00:06:06,800
and then I will
let you all explain

129
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:09,369
- his lack of options, okay?
- Yes, sir.

130
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:11,338
[Mullins]
I just want to sit back

131
00:06:11,439 --> 00:06:13,240
and watch.

132
00:06:14,041 --> 00:06:16,243
Have a seat, Ryan.

133
00:06:18,979 --> 00:06:22,550
[grunts] You said "breakfast,"
not "breakfast meeting."

134
00:06:22,683 --> 00:06:25,218
Every meal is a meeting
in this town, Ryan.

135
00:06:25,352 --> 00:06:27,120
- You know that.
- [Olson] Yeah.

136
00:06:28,255 --> 00:06:30,424
So, what is this meeting about?

137
00:06:30,558 --> 00:06:34,562
Uh, I'm on Foreign Relations,
not Intelligence.

138
00:06:34,662 --> 00:06:35,896
[Meade]
We had an agent come in

139
00:06:35,996 --> 00:06:37,498
from the cold.

140
00:06:38,331 --> 00:06:40,468
Come in from the cold?

141
00:06:40,568 --> 00:06:41,969
Mm-hmm.

142
00:06:42,069 --> 00:06:43,471
[Olson]
I'm not sure I follow you.

143
00:06:43,571 --> 00:06:45,138
[Mullins]
Not familiar with that term?

144
00:06:46,006 --> 00:06:46,974
I am not.

145
00:06:47,074 --> 00:06:49,109
[Westfield]
It's a reference

146
00:06:49,242 --> 00:06:50,978
to either one of our officers
who's been acting

147
00:06:51,078 --> 00:06:53,013
as a double agent
returning from that mission,

148
00:06:53,113 --> 00:06:56,116
or, in this instance,

149
00:06:56,216 --> 00:06:58,552
a Russian SVR agent
who has flipped

150
00:06:58,652 --> 00:07:01,254
and come to our side.

151
00:07:01,354 --> 00:07:04,257
- "Flipped."
- Defected.

152
00:07:04,357 --> 00:07:07,160
Hmm. Hmm.

153
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:08,896
And where is this agent now?

154
00:07:08,996 --> 00:07:11,899
We're holding them
at the Farm for now.

155
00:07:11,999 --> 00:07:13,433
After our debrief,
we're gonna move them

156
00:07:13,567 --> 00:07:15,803
into WitPro, most likely.

157
00:07:18,772 --> 00:07:20,107
Well...

158
00:07:20,941 --> 00:07:22,142
Good.

159
00:07:22,242 --> 00:07:24,311
I hope she gives you
some good intel.

160
00:07:24,444 --> 00:07:25,813
[chuckles]

161
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:29,282
- Something funny?
- Yeah, something's funny.

162
00:07:29,382 --> 00:07:30,851
[Olson]
Care to share?

163
00:07:30,951 --> 00:07:32,486
Well, you fucked up right there.

164
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:33,987
You know that, right?

165
00:07:34,121 --> 00:07:35,889
- Excuse me?
- I said,

166
00:07:35,989 --> 00:07:37,891
you fucked up.

167
00:07:37,991 --> 00:07:40,993
You can't talk to me like that.
I'm a sitting U.S. senator.

168
00:07:40,994 --> 00:07:42,195
Not for long.

169
00:07:43,296 --> 00:07:45,966
- Who is this?
- She's the senior case officer

170
00:07:46,066 --> 00:07:48,569
who was assigned to you.

171
00:07:49,703 --> 00:07:52,139
"Assigned" to me? To do what?

172
00:07:52,272 --> 00:07:55,475
[Joe]
To oversee your resignation.

173
00:07:56,476 --> 00:07:57,810
But let's back up a little bit.

174
00:07:57,811 --> 00:07:59,679
Let's talk about
how you fucked up.

175
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:01,514
Enlighten me.

176
00:08:01,515 --> 00:08:02,683
Sure.

177
00:08:02,816 --> 00:08:05,152
We said "foreign agent."

178
00:08:06,186 --> 00:08:07,487
You said "she."

179
00:08:07,621 --> 00:08:10,690
How'd you know
the agent is female?

180
00:08:10,691 --> 00:08:13,360
♪ slow, tense music ♪

181
00:08:14,161 --> 00:08:16,129
How'd you know that, Ryan?

182
00:08:16,964 --> 00:08:18,766
How'd you know?

183
00:08:18,866 --> 00:08:20,533
[Meade] The agent in question
provided us,

184
00:08:20,534 --> 00:08:22,702
among others,
you as one of her assets.

185
00:08:22,703 --> 00:08:25,072
- [scoffs] That's ridiculous.
- Oh, really?

186
00:08:25,172 --> 00:08:27,007
Well, she also provided us
with wire transfers

187
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:29,509
to an account
in the Altajir Bank branch,

188
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:30,978
George Town, Cayman Islands,

189
00:08:31,078 --> 00:08:34,181
under the name St. Paul
Alliance Ventures, LLC.

190
00:08:34,281 --> 00:08:37,183
S.P.A.V. then made
sizable contributions

191
00:08:37,184 --> 00:08:38,886
to various nonprofits, uh,

192
00:08:39,019 --> 00:08:41,688
PTP, Emerge, Think Left,

193
00:08:41,689 --> 00:08:44,524
who then made contributions
to your campaign.

194
00:08:44,625 --> 00:08:46,794
Which is a violation
of Title 18,

195
00:08:46,894 --> 00:08:50,197
United States Code Section 793.

196
00:08:50,330 --> 00:08:53,100
Which is treason, Ryan.

197
00:08:58,271 --> 00:09:00,608
[Westfield clears throat]
What we are here to do

198
00:09:00,708 --> 00:09:02,542
is present you with two options.

199
00:09:02,643 --> 00:09:06,279
Option one:
you resign, tomorrow.

200
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:09,382
Not in three days, not even two.

201
00:09:09,516 --> 00:09:10,751
Tomorrow.

202
00:09:10,851 --> 00:09:13,085
You will publicly resign
at this press conference.

203
00:09:13,086 --> 00:09:15,756
You will not seek any work
in the private sector,

204
00:09:15,889 --> 00:09:18,258
industries of defense,
media, education,

205
00:09:18,358 --> 00:09:20,260
lobbying in any form.

206
00:09:20,393 --> 00:09:23,997
You may not seek any office
on the state or municipal level.

207
00:09:24,097 --> 00:09:26,099
You may not practice law
and you will not sit

208
00:09:26,199 --> 00:09:28,636
on the board
of any publicly traded company.

209
00:09:29,436 --> 00:09:32,571
Option two:
you refuse option one

210
00:09:32,572 --> 00:09:35,242
or violate
any of option one's parameters,

211
00:09:35,342 --> 00:09:37,277
and that is your future.

212
00:09:37,377 --> 00:09:40,113
You will be arrested
and prosecuted

213
00:09:40,213 --> 00:09:42,315
to the fullest extent
of the law.

214
00:09:42,415 --> 00:09:43,951
The government will seize
all of your assets

215
00:09:44,084 --> 00:09:45,918
for nonpayment of taxes
on the funds

216
00:09:45,919 --> 00:09:47,454
that you accepted illegally.

217
00:09:47,554 --> 00:09:49,957
We will ensure that your family
shares in the suffering,

218
00:09:50,057 --> 00:09:52,159
as they most certainly
benefited from the crimes

219
00:09:52,259 --> 00:09:54,627
that you took
against this nation.

220
00:09:54,628 --> 00:09:58,031
And we will release
your offenses to the public

221
00:09:58,131 --> 00:09:59,332
and do irreparable harm

222
00:09:59,432 --> 00:10:01,969
to your name
and your family's name.

223
00:10:03,403 --> 00:10:05,172
Choice is yours.

224
00:10:06,173 --> 00:10:08,041
But we need your choice
and we need it now

225
00:10:08,141 --> 00:10:09,609
or we choose for you.

226
00:10:15,582 --> 00:10:18,952
What reason do I give
for resigning?

227
00:10:19,052 --> 00:10:21,288
- What's the reason?
- I don't care.

228
00:10:21,388 --> 00:10:23,791
- It's your decision.
- [stammers]

229
00:10:23,891 --> 00:10:25,092
What do I tell my family?

230
00:10:25,192 --> 00:10:26,894
[Meade]
I would suggest that you lie.

231
00:10:26,994 --> 00:10:28,595
Seems to come
naturally to you.

232
00:10:28,696 --> 00:10:30,798
[Westfield]
Say it's for health reasons.

233
00:10:30,931 --> 00:10:33,633
Then go to your doctor
and fucking find one.

234
00:10:33,734 --> 00:10:35,368
[Meade]
There you go.

235
00:10:36,269 --> 00:10:38,171
[Mullins]
Look at me, Ryan.

236
00:10:43,744 --> 00:10:45,846
Sinking in, isn't it?

237
00:10:48,816 --> 00:10:50,217
Yeah.

238
00:10:59,026 --> 00:11:01,561
Tomorrow, Ryan.

239
00:11:02,329 --> 00:11:04,564
We'll be watching.

240
00:11:05,665 --> 00:11:06,900
[Meade]
Great.

241
00:11:15,008 --> 00:11:17,044
There is a third option.

242
00:11:21,414 --> 00:11:23,483
You could kill yourself.

243
00:11:23,583 --> 00:11:25,853
♪ dark, tense music ♪

244
00:11:27,287 --> 00:11:28,889
That gets my vote.

245
00:11:37,798 --> 00:11:39,399
Think about it.

246
00:11:48,041 --> 00:11:50,310
♪ dramatic, haunting music ♪

247
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:23,110
♪

248
00:12:48,668 --> 00:12:50,971
♪ atmospheric, pulsing music ♪

249
00:13:15,128 --> 00:13:16,897
♪

250
00:13:28,842 --> 00:13:30,310
[engine starts]

251
00:13:34,481 --> 00:13:36,816
Oh, he's looking right at you.

252
00:13:36,917 --> 00:13:38,351
Yeah.

253
00:13:42,289 --> 00:13:44,657
Guess this ain't a soft tail.

254
00:13:44,757 --> 00:13:45,993
Nah.

255
00:13:46,093 --> 00:13:48,828
We want him to know:
where he goes, we go.

256
00:13:52,765 --> 00:13:54,701
[engine revs]

257
00:14:03,510 --> 00:14:05,879
[weatherman] <i>We have 55 degrees
currently for Downtown Metro,</i>

258
00:14:06,013 --> 00:14:07,346
